pr_comments:
> Even if we think you can disable it by editing a unit, we should make sure our tests cover this well enough (it would be bad to ship a kubelet that crashloops within system even when otherwise healthy).

It makes sense. If there is a strange error when systemd says it's enabled, but it is not really enabled, and kubelet fails later on, we may want to have a way to disable this code completely. 

@zhifei92 does this loop starts before we read the config or after? I wonder if we should hide all the new logic under the feature gate or introduce an environment variable for it.

review_comments:
> Optionally, notify `STATUS` to report the unhealthiness (and overwrite that once the kubelet is healthy again).

This is good, as it allows we to use `systemctl status kubelet.service`to see what happened with the kubelet.

@sftim It might be better not to add STATUS now. After testing locally, I found that the unhealthy state does not last very long. Once the kubelet is restarted by systemd, this state disappears, often leading to the unhealthy state being ignored. On the other hand, the healthy state is more common, but without using the watchdog, there is currently no healthy-related status. This might confuse users, and while we can explain this in the documentation, users might still overlook it.
